Boudewijn Waijers wrote on Sat, 1 Jan 2005 11:20:21 +0100:
> Note, however, that when a spellbook crumbles to dust as you read it,
> it's usually because it's too high level for you, not because it is
> cursed. So, writing a new one would only make sense when you wrote a
> blessed one, since you'd probably be unable to read it *again*. 

That's simply a far too conservative view, akin to saying that if you 
miss when you attack a monster, the monster was probably too high-level 
and you'd miss again if you attacked again.

The spellbook reading formula has a large random factor in it.  Failure 
normally means you simply got a bad roll of the die and should try again 
(you've price-ID'd the book, right?).

A level 6 Monk with 10 INT has only a 75% chance of successfully reading 
a level 1 spellbook on the first try, but I wouldn't let that stop me 
from trying repeatedly.  The immediate benefit of a new spell (hopefully 
it isn't Knock) far outweighs the possible risk of destroying the book.  

Waiting for holy water has its own set of risks.  Waiting until the 
chance is 100% is simply not reasonable -- our Monk needs 10 more levels 
or 5 more points of INT.

> And even then, you probably wouldn't be able to *cast* the spell in any case.

In my experience it is easier to cast spells than to learn them.  If one 
has a robe, like the Monk in our example, most spells will be at 0% fail.  
Anyway, one doesn't need a 0% fail rate in a spell to make use of it, 
just as one doesn't need a 100% hit rate with a weapon to make use of it.



